{{!-- V2 part: single root element; the sheet root form + classes come from DEFAULT_OPTIONS. --}}
<div class="sheet-wrapper" data-season="{{stonetop.season.key}}">

	{{!-- The ledger: every rating stated outright, on one row that shrinks to its contents. The
	      ladders the book prints are a pencil-and-paper technique — a stepper says the same thing
	      in a fraction of the height, and says the value out loud while it's at it. --}}
	{{!-- The ledger rail.

	      The ratings live in a column rather than a top band, for three reasons: the book draws
	      Fortunes and Surplus as ARCHES, which are a portrait form a horizontal strip can only
	      shrink; a column has no wrap to go ragged at any width; and the tab content beside it is
	      text-heavy, so being narrowed to a readable measure improves it. It is the same persistent
	      rail the character sheet already uses for its moves.

	      Order is identity → stores → ratings → conditions → mode: who this is, then what it has,
	      then how it is doing, then what is wrong, then how it rolls. --}}
	{{!-- The season's tint, as a rule above the ledger. Decorative, so it is hidden from assistive
	      tech: the season is STATED as text on the ledger line below and on the Season tab's wheel,
	      and a colour is never the only carrier of anything. It takes its tint from the season on
	      the wrapper, and falls back to the sheet accent for a season it has no colour for. --}}
	<div class="steading-season-band" aria-hidden="true"></div>

	{{!-- The rail, and everything beside it.

	      The rail owns the two ratings the book CROWNS and the moves you roll; the band owns the four
	      it rules plainly, plus everything conditional. Divided by kind, so "where is Prosperity" and
	      "where is Fortunes" each have exactly one answer.

	      It earns its 220px because of the MOVES, not the numbers: rolling a homefront move is the
	      thing this sheet is asked to do most, and it used to cost a tab change each way.

	      It opens ABOVE the band, so it runs from the season rule down past the tab rule — the arches
	      hold the top-left corner and the masthead and the body read as one object rather than as a
	      header stacked on content.

	      Left, mirroring the character sheet's right-hand moves rail. The steading's rail IS the
	      steading — the arches belong to the masthead's left edge — where the character's is a
	      reference index. Same component, `data-side` apart. --}}
	<div class="stonetop-rail-layout" data-side="left">

		{{!-- The toggle carries BOTH labels and the sheet picks between them as the state changes: a
		      control whose only tooltip is a noun never says it does anything, which is the reason a
		      shut rail was unreadable. The wording names what is in the rail — Fortunes, Surplus, the
		      homefront moves — rather than the book's word for the illustration around them, which
		      means nothing to a reader who has not read this file. The strip shows the same two
		      things as marks: the arch it is drawn in, and the die you roll a move on. --}}
		<button type="button" class="stonetop-rail-toggle" data-action="toggleRail" data-view-state
		        aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-rail"
		        data-label-show="{{localize 'stonetop.steading.railShow'}}"
		        data-label-hide="{{localize 'stonetop.steading.railHide'}}"
		        title="{{localize 'stonetop.steading.railShow'}}"
		        aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.steading.railShow'}}">
			<i class="fas fa-chevron-left stonetop-rail-caret" aria-hidden="true"></i>
			<span class="stonetop-rail-fold" aria-hidden="true">
				<i class="fas fa-archway stonetop-rail-mark"></i>
				<i class="fas fa-dice-d6 stonetop-rail-mark"></i>
			</span>
		</button>

		<div class="stonetop-rail steading-rail" id="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-rail" data-density="full">
			{{!-- The book gives Fortunes and Surplus an illustrated arch and the other four a plain
			      rule. This is the one place on the sheet with the portrait room to honour that. --}}
			<div class="steading-archpair">
				{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="fortunes" attrData=stonetop.fortunes rollable=true changeAction="fortunes" panelClass="steading-fortunes steading-tile--arched"}}
				{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="surplus"  attrData=stonetop.surplus                changeAction="surplus"  panelClass="steading-surplus steading-tile--arched"}}
			</div>
			{{#if stonetop.homefrontMoves}}
				{{> "stonetop.move-group"
				    title=stonetop.homefrontMoves.label
				    note=stonetop.homefrontMoves.note
				    moves=stonetop.homefrontMoves.moves
				    categoryKey=stonetop.homefrontMoves.key
				    allowAdditional=false
				    alwaysShowResource=true
				    unacquirable=true
				    disclosure=true}}
			{{/if}}
			{{!-- The four Seasons Change moves, as a second group of the same kind. They used to hang
			      off the wheel's segments, which put a whole move inside a control whose job is to
			      say what time of year it is — and put them on one tab, reachable only from there.
			      The rail is where this steading's moves are read and rolled, so they are here, beside
			      the homefront ones, at every width and on every tab.

			      The season the steading is IN is still performed in the Season tab's box, as the
			      numbered steps the move prescribes. A move is not a thing the sheet rations: both
			      renderings go through the same row, so it rolls and posts identically in either. --}}
			{{#if stonetop.seasonalMoves}}
				{{> "stonetop.move-group"
				    title=stonetop.seasonalMoves.label
				    note=stonetop.seasonalMoves.note
				    moves=stonetop.seasonalMoves.moves
				    categoryKey=stonetop.seasonalMoves.key
				    allowAdditional=false
				    alwaysShowResource=true
				    unacquirable=true
				    disclosure=true}}
			{{/if}}
		</div>

	<div class="stonetop-rail-main steading-main">

	{{!-- The ledger line.

	      One component, two densities: this same markup is what the Play tab renders at full size,
	      with the arch pair and the evidence lists. It is NOT a second, compact copy — if the two
	      ever drift apart the "one component" claim is gone and this is just two headers. The
	      density is a class on the container; nothing here knows which one it is in.

	      Every rating's name is its roll button in both densities, which is what lets you roll from
	      the line without a second code path to keep in step. --}}
	<header class="sheet-header steading-line" data-density="line">

		{{!-- Row one: who this is, and every number. Row two: what is wrong, and how it rolls.

		      Two rows in the MARKUP rather than a flex-basis trick, because they are two different
		      statements and the rule between them is what says so. Rolling a steading move needs a
		      rating, the condition bending it and the roll mode — so all three stay here, on every
		      tab, and the tab below scrolls under them rather than taking them with it. --}}
		<div class="steading-line-row steading-line-values">

		<div class="steading-identity">
			{{!-- Name combobox: type the steading's own name, or pick a steadfast from the list (which
			      seeds its profile and adopts its name). Dropping a steadfast onto the sheet does the same. --}}
			<span class="stonetop-combo steading-steadfast-combo">
				<input type="text" class="steading-steadfast-input stonetop-combo-input" data-change-action="steadfastName" value="{{actor.name}}"
				       aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.steadingName'}}"
				       placeholder="{{localize 'stonetop.steadfast.namePlaceholder'}}" {{#unless editable}}disabled{{/unless}}>
				{{#if availableSteadfasts.length}}
				<button type="button" class="stonetop-combo-toggle" tabindex="-1" aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.steading.steadfastHint'}}">▾</button>
				<ul class="stonetop-combo-list" hidden>
					{{#each availableSteadfasts}}<li class="stonetop-combo-option" data-value="{{name}}">{{name}}</li>{{/each}}
				</ul>
				{{/if}}
			</span>

			{{!-- Size is a category, not a quantity, so it reads with the name — "Stonetop, a village" —
			      rather than sitting in a row of numbers pretending to be one. Its own shape, for the
			      same reason: a pill, not a value on a rule. --}}
			{{> "stonetop.steading-size-pill" attrData=stonetop.attributes.size}}

		</div>

		{{!-- The quantities.

		      Fortunes and Surplus are the rail's, and the rail is present at every width the sheet is
		      wide enough for. They are rendered HERE regardless and hidden by a container query while
		      the rail is showing them — so when the rail drawers, the two ratings it was carrying are
		      already on the line rather than one class-toggle away from existing. One partial, one code
		      path, and "collapsing must not hide the ratings" holds without anything having to run.

		      They lead the line, as the book prints them. --}}
		<div class="steading-ledger">
			{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="fortunes"   attrData=stonetop.fortunes              rollable=true changeAction="fortunes" panelClass="steading-fortunes steading-railed"}}
			{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="surplus"    attrData=stonetop.surplus                             changeAction="surplus"  panelClass="steading-surplus steading-railed"}}
			{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="population" attrData=stonetop.attributes.population rollable=true panelClass="steading-population"}}
			{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="prosperity" attrData=stonetop.attributes.prosperity rollable=true panelClass="steading-prosperity"}}
			{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="defenses"   attrData=stonetop.attributes.defenses   rollable=true panelClass="steading-defenses"}}
		</div>

		{{!-- The season, in words, with its own glyph beside them. Display only: advancing it runs a
		      whole turnover, so that control lives on the Season tab and nowhere else — an
		      advance-the-season click reachable from anywhere is a foot-gun on a sheet six people can
		      edit. It ends the values row rather than the conditions row: the season is a fact about
		      the steading, like every number beside it, not a thing you set before rolling. The glyph
		      is decoration — image and tint both come from the `data-season` on the wrapper, and the
		      words next to it already say which season it is. --}}
		<p class="steading-season-line">
			<span class="steading-season-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
			{{localize "stonetop.steading.seasons.stated"
			           season=(localize stonetop.season.labelKey) year=stonetop.year}}
		</p>

		</div>{{!-- /row one --}}

		{{!-- Row two. Conditions always say what they do — a steading marks these rarely, and when it
		      does, "what does diminished mean again?" is the very next question at the table, so the
		      effect is text in the DOM, never a pointer-only tooltip. The roll mode ends the row
		      because it is the last thing you set before rolling. --}}
		<div class="steading-line-row steading-line-conditions">

		<div class="steading-conditions">
			{{#each stonetop.debilities}}
				<label class="steading-debility{{#if active}} is-active{{/if}}">
					<input class="steading-circle-input" data-change-action="debility" type="checkbox" data-slug="{{slug}}" {{#if active}}checked{{/if}}>
					<span class="steading-circle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
					<span class="steading-debility-text">
						<span class="steading-debility-name">{{name}}</span>
						<span class="steading-debility-effect"><span class="steading-debility-dash" aria-hidden="true">—</span> {{effect}}</span>
					</span>
				</label>
			{{/each}}
		</div>

		{{!-- A mode, not a value: no rule and no box, just the active word in the accent. Last on the
		      line, pushed to the far end — it modifies a roll rather than describing the steading. --}}
		<fieldset class="steading-rollmode">
			<legend class="steading-line-heading">{{localize "stonetop.rollMode.label"}}</legend>
			{{#each stonetop.rollModes}}
				<label class="steading-rollmode-option{{#if checked}} is-checked{{/if}}">
					<input type="radio" class="steading-rollmode-input" name="stonetop-roll-mode"
					       data-change-action="rollMode" value="{{key}}" {{#if checked}}checked{{/if}}>
					<span>{{localize labelKey}}</span>
				</label>
			{{/each}}
		</fieldset>

		</div>{{!-- /row two --}}

	</header>

	{{!-- Tab navigation. data-action="tab" is core ApplicationV2's built-in action (→ changeTab); each
	      button carries data-group because changeTab addresses `.tabs [data-group][data-tab]` directly.
	      The ARIA and the arrow-key roving are ours — core ships neither. --}}
	<nav class="sheet-tabs tabs" data-group="primary" role="tablist" aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.steadingTabs'}}">
		{{#each tabs}}
		<button type="button" class="item {{this.cssClass}}" data-action="tab" data-group="primary" data-tab="{{this.id}}"
		        role="tab" id="{{@root.sheetIdPrefix}}-tab-{{this.id}}" aria-controls="{{@root.sheetIdPrefix}}-panel-{{this.id}}"
		        aria-selected="{{#if this.active}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}" tabindex="{{#if this.active}}0{{else}}-1{{/if}}">{{localize this.label}}</button>
		{{/each}}
		{{!-- One ? for the sheet, at the end of the tab row. Every rating's advice is one page of the
		      same article, and its headings are all on it — so six buttons opening the same document
		      at six anchors is five buttons too many. --}}
		{{> "stonetop.advice-button" variant="sheet"}}
	</nav>

	<section class="sheet-body">

		{{!-- Play — the tab you are on essentially the whole session.

		      The ratings at their full density: the SAME tile partial the line renders, laid out so a
		      rating leads the evidence that justifies it — Prosperity over Resources, Defenses over
		      Fortifications. That pairing is the book's best structural idea.

		      Two columns, not three: Fortunes and Surplus lead no list, so they went to the rail with
		      the moves, and each column here is now wider than all three were. --}}
		<div class="tab {{tabs.play.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="play"
		     role="tabpanel" id="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-panel-play" aria-labelledby="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-tab-play">

			<div class="steading-overview-grid steading-play-grid" data-density="full">

				<section class="steading-overview-column">
					{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="prosperity" attrData=stonetop.attributes.prosperity rollable=true panelClass="steading-prosperity"}}
					{{> "stonetop.steading-ratings-list" title=(localize "stonetop.steading.lists.resources") addLabel=(localize "stonetop.steading.lists.addResource") attr="prosperity" items=stonetop.attributes.prosperity.items}}
					{{!-- The steading's own scratchpad.

					      Here rather than with the safety agreements it used to sit beside: it is
					      written to mid-scene, on the tab the table is on all session, and a
					      scratchpad filed next to a content policy is what made that tab read as a
					      drawer of leftovers. It is also what LEVELS the two columns, which are
					      different lengths — it is the one thing on the tab that can use the height it
					      is given, so it takes the slack and the plate below it simply closes the
					      column. --}}
					<div class="steading-notes-field steading-block">
						{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.steading.headings.notes")}}
						{{!-- The same field as the note kept on a neighbouring place: a framed box that grows
						      with what is written, and a placeholder saying what goes on it. Nothing here draws
						      the frame — it is the one core gives a textarea, kept rather than stripped, which
						      is what makes the two boxes the same object without either of them describing it.
						      `stonetop-notes` stays FIRST in the list — buildFocusSelector rebuilds the caret's
						      selector from the first stonetop-/steading- class it finds, and the grower is a
						      shared idiom that would match every note on the sheet. --}}
						<textarea aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.notes'}}"
						          class="stonetop-notes stonetop-grow-field"
						          placeholder="{{localize 'stonetop.steading.headings.notes'}}"
						          data-change-action="notes">{{stonetop.notes}}</textarea>
					</div>
					{{!-- The book's whisky jugs, closing the column the resources fill — the one line of that
					      list the book itself illustrates, under the list that names it. Absent in a world
					      that has never run the art installer, which is what the snapshot answering null
					      rather than a path is for. --}}
					{{#if stonetop.resourcesPlate}}
						<figure class="steading-resources-plate">
							<img src="{{stonetop.resourcesPlate}}" alt="">
						</figure>
					{{/if}}
				</section>

				<section class="steading-overview-column">
					{{> "stonetop.steading-stat-panel" attr="defenses" attrData=stonetop.attributes.defenses rollable=true panelClass="steading-defenses"}}
					{{> "stonetop.steading-ratings-list" title=(localize "stonetop.steading.lists.fortifications") addLabel=(localize "stonetop.steading.lists.addFortification") attr="defenses" items=stonetop.attributes.defenses.items}}
					{{> "stonetop.steading-assets" items=stonetop.assets.items
				    note=stonetop.assets.requisitionedNote requisition=true}}
					{{> "stonetop.steading-coinage" coinage=stonetop.assets.coinage advice="coin"}}
				</section>

			</div>

		</div>{{!-- /tab play --}}

		{{!-- Folk — one roster of the people of and around the steading, beside the lists you scan
		      to invent them --}}
		<div class="tab {{tabs.folk.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="folk"
		     role="tabpanel" id="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-panel-folk" aria-labelledby="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-tab-folk">

			{{!-- One roster, and the lists you read to fill it.

			      Two columns rather than the four stacked blocks this used to be: the roster on the left,
			      and on the right the name and trait lists WHOLE. The search box narrows the roster and
			      cannot reach the lists — reading down them is how an NPC gets made, so filtering them
			      would remove the feature to speed up the overhead. --}}
			<div class="steading-folk-grid">

				{{> "stonetop.steading-folk-roster" folk=stonetop.folk isGM=isGM plate=stonetop.residentsPlate}}

				<div class="steading-folk-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

				<section class="steading-folk-ref steading-block">
					{{#each stonetop.folkSuggestions}}
						{{> "stonetop.steading-folk-suggestions" list=this}}
					{{/each}}
				</section>

			</div>

		</div>{{!-- /tab folk --}}

		{{!-- Places — the map, at both its scales: the landmarks inside the walls and the communities
		      outside them. The neighbours' NAME pools stay on Folk, in the reference column, because
		      inventing a stranger from Marshedge is a Folk job; what the steading knows ABOUT
		      Marshedge is this one. --}}
		<div class="tab {{tabs.places.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="places"
		     role="tabpanel" id="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-panel-places" aria-labelledby="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-tab-places">

			<div class="steading-split-grid">

				<section class="steading-split-column">
					{{> "stonetop.steading-places-of-interest" places=stonetop.placesOfInterest linkHint=true}}
				</section>

				<div class="steading-split-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

				<section class="steading-split-column">
					{{> "stonetop.steading-neighbor-places" title=(localize "stonetop.steading.headings.neighbors")
					   places=stonetop.neighborPlaces showNames=false showTravel=true}}
				</section>

			</div>

		</div>{{!-- /tab places --}}

		{{!-- Season — the ritual: the Seasons Change move, the gains picker, and the improvement board --}}
		<div class="tab {{tabs.season.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="season"
		     role="tabpanel" id="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-panel-season" aria-labelledby="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-tab-season">

			<section class="sheet-tab">
				{{> "stonetop.steading-seasons" seasons=stonetop.seasons}}
			</section>

			<div class="stonetop-advice-toolbar">
				{{> "stonetop.advice-button" variant="labelled" topic="steadingImprovement"}}
			</div>

			{{> "stonetop.steading-improvement-board" board=stonetop.improvements}}

		</div>{{!-- /tab season --}}

		{{!-- Content — the table's own agreements about what this game holds, and the book's procedure
		      for changing them. The tab is named for what it keeps: "Chronicle" named a record it no
		      longer holds, and a scratchpad filed beside a safety policy is what made it read as a
		      drawer of leftovers. The places of interest it used to lead with are on Places now, and
		      the steading's notes on Play, where they are written to.

		      The procedure stands BESIDE the lists rather than above them, because it is read while
		      they are — it is what the table reaches for mid-session, when someone has called time
		      out — and it is quoted whole: the book prints it on the playbook itself (Book I p. 155),
		      which makes it sheet copy rather than GM-chapter prose. --}}
		<div class="tab {{tabs.content.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="content"
		     role="tabpanel" id="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-panel-content" aria-labelledby="{{sheetIdPrefix}}-tab-content">

			<div class="steading-split-grid steading-content-grid">

				<section class="steading-split-column">
					{{#each stonetop.content}}
						{{> "stonetop.steading-content-list" section=this}}
					{{/each}}
				</section>

				<div class="steading-split-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

				<section class="steading-split-column">
					{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.steading.content.fromThePlaybook")}}
					<div class="steading-content-procedure stonetop-rich">{{rich (localize "stonetop.steading.content.procedure")}}</div>
				</section>

			</div>

		</div>{{!-- /tab content --}}

	</section>{{!-- /sheet-body --}}

	</div>{{!-- /rail main --}}
	</div>{{!-- /rail layout --}}

</div>
